// CATALOGUE DEPTH SEED
// ====================
//
// Breadth without depth is a different failure from depth without breadth, and
// the first pass at this catalogue had it. Spreading products across 125
// product types averaged six per type — and measurement showed exactly what
// that costs: types holding 15+ products returned strong competitive coverage
// for 100% of their products, while types holding 4–6 refused outright,
// because after the 0.6×–1.7× direct-competitor band there was nobody left to
// compare against.
//
// That was a DATA problem being mistaken for an engine problem. A real
// marketplace does not stock five ballpoint pens or five computer mice; it
// stocks hundreds, clustered tightly on price. This file supplies that density
// for the categories that genuinely have it.
//
// WHAT IS AND IS NOT DEEPENED
// ---------------------------
// Commodity and mass-market types are brought to 10–15 products in tight price
// bands, because that is what they look like. Genuinely thin markets —
// treadmills, action cameras, strollers, glucometers, sofas, wardrobes — are
// LEFT THIN on purpose. They are the fixtures that prove the engine refuses
// rather than invents when the market cannot support a recommendation, and
// padding them would destroy the only evidence that the refusal path works.

